{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, add a bounded frequency-change input feature: scale each nonpadding history embedding by 1+0.05*tanh(log(c_previous+1)-log(c_current+1)), using counts from supplied training interactions only; use scale 1 at the first real position. This may distinguish transitions toward niche versus common items without globally attenuating frequent items. Keep candidate embeddings, embedding parameterization, GRU, readout, loss and optimizer fixed. Store counts in each category checkpoint and reproduce the feature in the predictor writer.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, shorten only the causal pooling half-life from three interactions to two, retaining the incumbent 0.8*h_t+0.2*m_t readout. The improvement from four to three motivates testing slightly stronger recency. Exclude padding and future states, and read the final real position at inference. Preserve truncation, features, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer; synchronize readout metadata and the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, change only loss aggregation: average the existing BPR plus 0.2 pointwise loss over valid positions separately for each sampled user, then average those user losses across the batch. This gives short and long histories equal total weight, potentially improving user-averaged HR@10. Preserve the loss terms, features, embeddings, GRU, readout, scoring, optimizer, sampling, scheduler and batch/epoch settings.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, replace each tied unit-normalized item vector with normalize(v_i-mean_coordinates(v_i)), retaining 128 dimensions and zero padding. Removing each vector's coordinate mean may reduce a shared nuisance direction while preserving angular discrimination and tied input/output representations. Keep features, GRU topology, history policy, readout, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Apply the same embedding transformation in training and the generated predictor, with matching checkpoint metadata."
}
